"helpText" = "## How to use\n1. Copy text with a number (e.g. “Chapter 1”).\n2. Press **⌥V** where you want to paste: “Chapter 2” is pasted.\n3. The new value stays on the clipboard, so pressing ⌥V again pastes “Chapter 3”, “Chapter 4”…\n- To paste “Chapter 1” itself on the first press, turn on “Paste the copied text as it is on the first press” in Settings > Counting.\n\n## What is counted up (in this order)\n1. **Dates**: “10月13日”, “2026年10月13日”, “2026-10-13”, “2026/10/13”. Month ends and leap years are handled, and a following weekday such as “（火）” is updated. Without a year, this year is used.\n2. **The last number**: “1-2-3” → “1-2-4”, “1.2” → “1.3”, “Chapter 1-1” → “Chapter 1-2”. Leading zeros (001 → 002) and full-width digits are kept. Circled numbers ①–㊿ count too.\n3. **Letters** (when there is no number): “A” → “B”, “(A)” → “(B)”, “Z” → “AA”, case kept.\n- When there is none of these, the clipboard is pasted as it is and a message is shown.\n\n## Main window\n- Shows the clipboard and the value the next ⌥V pastes.\n- “Put Back the Previous Clipboard”: goes back to the value before the last count-up (not after you copy something else).\n\n## Settings\n- Startup & Background: open at login (off by default), hide the main window at startup, hide from Dock and App Switcher, keep running when the window is closed, menu bar icon, Accessibility status, restart and quit.\n- Hotkey: turn ⌥V on or off, Change… records a key with ⌘, ⌥ or ⌃ (Esc cancels), Reset to Default.\n- Counting: amount per press (1), dates and letters, the first press after a copy, on-screen value.\n- Excluded Apps: apps where ⌥V is left alone. Defaults: Illustrator, Excel, InDesign, ScreenFlow.\n- ⌘, opens Settings, ⌘W closes it.\n\n## Hotkey\n- **⌥V**: count up and paste.\n- While an excluded app is in front, the key is not registered and works as that app intends.\n- To stop it for a while, choose “Pause” from the menu bar icon.\n\n## Permission\n- **Accessibility**: needed to send Paste (⌘V) to the front app. Turn on PasteMaestro in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Accessibility.\n\n## About the clipboard\n- When a value is counted up, the clipboard is replaced with it (plain text) so the next ⌥V continues. Use “Put Back the Previous Clipboard” to go back.\n- When nothing is counted up, the clipboard is not changed.\n\n## Running and quitting\n- PasteMaestro stays in the menu bar; ⌥V works with the window closed.\n- The menu bar icon, the Dock icon, opening the app again or ⌘0 shows the main window.\n- With “Hide from Dock and App Switcher” on in Settings, the app appears in the Dock and ⌘Tab while main/settings windows exist, including minimized windows, and hides after all such windows close. Show it from the menu bar icon or by opening the app again in Finder.\n- Quit from the menu bar icon, or ⌘Q while the app is in front.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- **Nothing happens**: check Accessibility, that the hotkey is on, and that the front app is not excluded.\n- **The wrong number changes**: the last number is counted up; dates are moved as dates (dates can be turned off).\n- **“√” is typed**: ⌥V is not registered; check the hotkey status in Settings.\n\n## Sharing settings\n- In Settings › Sharing, Export & Import saves the settings to a file (PasteMaestro-Settings.plist) or reads them back, for moving to another Mac or keeping a backup. Importing asks before replacing.\n- Settings Sync keeps the settings in a folder such as iCloud Drive or Dropbox; Macs that choose the same folder share the settings, and the most recent save wins.\n- Shared: the hotkey and the counting-up options. Launch at login, staying open, the menu bar icon and excluded apps stay on each Mac.\n\n## Privacy\n- Everything runs on this Mac and nothing is sent out (except update checks).\n- Clipboard text is never saved to files or logs.\n\n## Dock visibility and application menus\n\nMain and settings windows keep the app in the Dock and ⌘Tab so its application menus remain available. Minimized windows stay listed. Close all such windows to hide it; palettes alone do not show it. Your saved setting is preserved.";
